"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[6295],{6295:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"physiology-test-2","slug":"physiology","subject":"Physiology","testNo":2,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Physiology","text":"Which of the following oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curves corresponds to blood during resting conditions (dark blue line) and blood during exercise ( light blue line)?","options":["Graph B","Graph A","Graph D","Graph C"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Graph B\\n\\nIn the exercise, several factors shift the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, which serves to deliver extra amounts of O2 to the exercising muscle.\\n\\nThese factors include increasing the amount of CO2 released from the muscle tissue, increased H+ concentration in the muscle capillary blood, and increased temperature resulting from heat generated by the exercising muscle.\\n\\nThe right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve allows more oxygen to be released to the muscle at a given Po2 in the blood.\\n\\nOxygen-Hb dissociation curve (OHDC)\\n\\n• This is a\xa0plot of the partial pressure of O2\xa0and the % saturation of Hb with O2.\\n• It is normally\xa0sigmoid-shape.\\n• If OHDC is shifted to the right, it means that affinity of Hb for O2\xa0has become less (which favors O2\xa0delivery) i.e. Higher the P50. (R - Right shift\xa0Releases oxygen)\\n\\nShift to the right is caused by:\xa0(Conditions with increased requirement of O2 - metabolically active tissues)\\n\\n• Hypoxia and Hypercarbia - Metabolically active tissues utilise O2\xa0and tissues produce CO2\\n• ↓ pH - Secondary to increased CO2\xa0production\\n• ↑ in temperature, heat\\n• ↑ in 2,3-DPG - increased anaerobic glycolysis\\n• Growth hormone, thyroid hormone, androgen - by increasing 2,3 DPG\\n• Sickle cell anaemia - reduced affinity for O2","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4ce0c21a7c9a8237a0c7.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0a382afe1de820a7d132.jpg"}]},{"n":2,"subject":"Physiology","text":"In ORS, sodium is given along with glucose to facilitate___?","options":["Primary active transport","Osmosis","Facilitated diffusion","Co-transport"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Co-transport\\n\\nIn ORS, sodium is given along with glucose to facilitate\xa0co-transport.\\n\\nGlucose co transported with sodium is an example of Secondary Active transport.\\n\\nThe glucose-dependent sodium and water absorption is the principle behind replacing glucose and sodium in a 1:1 molar ratio in the WHO oral rehydration solution (ORS). The low osmolarity of the ORS in the lumen as compared to the blood facilitate absorption of fluid and hence the electrolytes due to the solvent drag.\\n\\nThe\xa0low-osmolality\xa0World Health Organization (WHO) oral rehydration solution (ORS) is now the global\xa0standard of care\xa0in the management of acute gastroenteritis.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nTypes of transport:\\n\\n• Active transport:\\n\\n• Primary\\n\\n• Energy is derived from the transporter itself. Eg Na+ K+ ATPase pump.\\n\\n• Secondary:\\n\\n• Energy is derived indirectly. Eg. SGLT – Sodium linked glucose transport\\n\\n• Passive transport\\n\\n• Simple:\\n\\n• No energy is required.\\n• No carrier molecule is involved.\\n• Eg. O2 exchange in alveoli.\\n\\n• Facilitated:\\n\\n• No energy is required.\\n• Carrier molecule involved.\\n• Eg. Glucose transport by GLUT transporters.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":3,"subject":"Physiology","text":"The following process where transportation is along the concentration gradient is an example of:","options":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Primary active transport","Secondary active transport"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Facilitated diffusion\\n\\nThe given image shows a glucose transporter that transports glucose from a higher concentration to a lower concentration. This is an example of facilitated diffusion.\\n\\nWhen carrier proteins help in the movement of substances in the direction of their chemical or electrical gradients (downhill movement), no energy input is required and the process is called facilitated diffusion.\\n\\nGlucose transporters also referred to as GLUTs, are transport proteins in the cell membrane responsible for the transfer of glucose into the cells by facilitated diffusion. Facilitated diffusion involves the passage of molecules or ions inside the cell membrane by binding to a carrier/ transport protein.\\n\\nFor example, insulin stimulates glucose entry into the cells via facilitated diffusion by increasing the number of glucose transporters (GLUTs) in the cell membrane.\xa0This type of insulin-dependent glucose transport via GLUT 4 is observed in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and adipose tissues.\\n\\nOther options -\\n\\nOption A. Simple diffusion -\xa0It is along the concentration gradient but is not carrier-mediated.\xa0In the cell, examples of molecules that can use simple diffusion to travel in and out of the cell membrane are water, oxygen, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen. They pass directly through the cell membrane without energy along the concentration gradient.\\n\\nOption C.\xa0Primary active transport - It is a form of cellular transport requiring energy in the form of\xa0hydrolysis of ATP\xa0to ship and transfer molecules against a membrane with chemical or electrical gradient differences.\xa0The important examples of primary active transport include sodium-potassium ATPase, proton pump, and calcium ATPase.\\n\\nOption D. In secondary active transport,\xa0the active transport of sodium is coupled to the transport of other substances. Examples include symport of sodium with glucose (luminal membranes of mucosal cells in the small intestine), sodium-potassium ATPase, and Ca2+\xa0in the cardiac muscle cells.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/db77f07d892cd8bd4bc0.jpg"]},{"n":4,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A 40-year-old man presents to the hospital with complaints of irritability, increased sweating, and weight loss of around 6 kgs for the last 3 months. On examination, the patient has tachycardia and enlarged neck mass which moves with deglutition. An image of the patient is shown below. Which of the following statements are true regarding the hormone elevated in this patient?\\n\\n1. T4 has a longer half-life than T3\\n2. T3 is more in concentration than T4\\n3. This hormone binds to nuclear receptors\\n4. Iodine transporter is also present in pituitary gland\\n5. Drugs used in this condition primarily act by inhibiting deiodinase","options":["1 and 3","1, 4, and 5","2, 4, and 5","1, 2, and 3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1 and 3\\n\\nThe clinical features are suggestive of Graves disease. Thyroid hormone is elevated in this patient. Statements 1 and 3 are true regarding the thyroid hormone.\\n\\nSynthesis- Iodine is required for the synthesis of thyroid hormone. Absorbed iodine is taken by the thyroid follicular cells with the help of a sodium iodide transporter (NIS). NIS is mainly present in the thyroid gland, but it can also be present in\xa0salivary glands, lactating breast, and placenta.\xa0NIS is not present in the pituitary gland.\\n\\nThe iodine that enters the thyroid gland undergoes organification and is added to tyrosyl residues within thyroglobulin. These iodotyrosines are then coupled to form T4 and T3 hormones. This reaction is catalyzed by thyroid peroxidase. After coupling, the thyroglobulin is taken back into the cells to release T4 and T3. Uncoupled mono and diiodotyrosines are deiodinated by the enzyme dehalogenase to recycle iodine.\\n\\nT3 and T4 are the two forms of the hormone. T4 is present in higher concentrations and has a longer half-life than T3. However, T3 is more potent than T4.\\n\\nThyroid hormones enter the cell by passive diffusion or by the transporters. On entering the cell, they bind to the nuclear thyroid hormone receptors. On binding to the receptors, they either stimulate or inhibit transcription.\\n\\nMethimazole, carbimazole and propylthiouracil are the drugs used in the management of hyperthyroidism. They act by inhibiting thyroid peroxidase.\xa0In addition to this, propylthiouracil also inhibits the deiodination of T4 → T3.\\n\\nDrugs that prevent deiodination of T4 to T3 are propylthiouracil, propranolol, amiodarone, and\xa0oral cholecystographic agents.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/fa39cdd67142925db71e.png"]},{"n":5,"subject":"Physiology","text":"An obstetrician elicits a reflex by performing a procedure for labour induction on a patient as depicted in the image below. What type of homeostatic feedback mechanism is associated with this reflex?","options":["Negative feedback control","Anticipatory control","Feedforward control","Positve feedback control"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Positve feedback control\\n\\nThe procedure done in the image above to induce labour is the stripping of membranes which elicits the Ferguson reflex. Ferguson\'s reflex is associated with a positive feedback mechanism.\\n\\nStripping/Sweeping of membranes refers to the digital separation of the chorioamniotic membranes from the lower uterine segment and cervix wall. It is associated with an increase in blood levels of prostaglandin F2α metabolite, also known as dinoprost.\\n\\nPositive feedback is a type of feedback in which the initial stimulus triggers a response that deviates further from the original state.\\n\\nThe fetal ejection reflex (Ferguson reflex) is a phenomenon in which mechanical stretching of the internal cervix enhances uterine activity. Oxytocin is released as the fetal head presses on the uterine cervix, which further enhances uterine contraction. This finally helps in the delivery of the fetus by facilitating fetal head descent. It is an example of a positive feedback mechanism.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Negative feedback mechanism- If the activity of a particular system is increased, the controller system initiates the negative feedback mechanism, which prevents overactivity and ensures normal homeostasis (e.g., baroreceptor reflex).\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Feedforward control also called anticipatory control (option C), is that which makes an anticipatory or adaptive response without a specific stimulus. This is seen in body temperature regulation where\xa0thermoregulatory responses are initiated by the hypothalamus before the changes in environmental temperature change the core body temperature. Thus,\xa0shivering in response to a cold environment is an example of feedforward control.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/042f0bc1960ed1625a2d.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/747af9c865a3e95ded60.jpg"}]},{"n":6,"subject":"Physiology","text":"All of the following increases gastric acid secretion except:","options":["Histamine","Acetylcholine","Serotonin","Gastrin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Serotonin\\n\\nAll of the above increase gastric acid secretion except serotonin.\\n\\nSerotonin is localized in the enterochromaffin cells of the gastrointestinal (GI) mucosa and within neurons of the GI tract. Serotonin inhibits gastric acid secretion. It stimulates the production and release of gastric and colonic mucus. Serotonin acts as an enteric neurotransmitter and increases intestinal motility.\\n\\nThe main factors that stimulate gastric secretion are acetylcholine, gastrin, and histamine.\xa0Parasympathetic stimulation releases Ach which stimulates the secretion of\\n\\n• pepsinogen by peptic cells\\n• hydrochloric acid by parietal cells\\n• mucus by mucous cells.\\n\\nBoth gastrin and histamine strongly stimulate acid secretion by parietal cells\xa0but have little effect on other cells.\\n\\nThe stomach mucosa has two important types of tubular glands:\\n\\n• The oxyntic (gastric) glands secrete hydrochloric acid, pepsinogen, intrinsic factor, and mucus.\\n• The pyloric glands secrete mainly mucus for protection of the pyloric mucosa from the stomach acid. They also secrete the hormone gastrin.\\n\\nNote- Serotonin released from the small intestine triggers vomiting by stimulation of 5HT3 receptors. Therefore,\xa05HT3 antagonists like ondansetron are used as antiemetics.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":7,"subject":"Physiology","text":"The structures involved in the auditory pathway are -\\n\\n1. Superior olivary nucleus\\n2. Nucleus of the medial lemniscus\\n3. Superior colliculus\\n4. Medial geniculate body\\n5. Superior temporal gyrus","options":["1, 4 and 5","1, 2 and 3","3, 4 and 5","2, 3 and 4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1, 4 and 5\\n\\nThe structures involved in the auditory pathway are\xa01, 4, and 5, i.e, the\xa0superior olivary nucleus,\xa0medial geniculate body, and\xa0superior temporal gyrus.\\n\\nThe correct sequence of structures involved in the auditory pathway from the periphery to the center is -\\n\\n1. Vestibulocochlear nerve\\n2. Cochlear nucleus\\n3. Superior olivary nucleus\\n4. Lateral lemniscus\\n5. Inferior colliculus\\n6. Medial geniculate body\\n7. Auditory cortex - superior temporal gyrus","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3fb2422320d64f815b93.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0324fbfd5b817f11aff7.jpg"}]},{"n":8,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A person after having a meal had a sense of fullness and feels that he can no longer eat. Which area of the hypothalamus acts as the centre for the given physiological process?","options":["Supraoptic nucleus","Medial preoptic area","Ventromedial nucleus","Lateral hypothalamic area"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ventromedial nucleus\\n\\nThe physiological process explained in the question stem is that of satiety. The\xa0ventromedial nucleus\xa0of the hypothalamus acts as the centre for satiety.\\n\\nA centre that reduces the desire for food is known as a satiety centre. These centres are sensitive to blood glucose concentration\xa0and cause\xa0satiety in hyperglycemic states. The hypothalamic areas that regulate satiety are:\\n\\n• Ventromedial nucleus\\n• Arcuate nucleus (also acts as a centre for hunger)\\n• Paraventricular nucleus (also regulates oxytocin release and water conservation)\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Supraoptic nucleus\xa0regulates vasopressin release.\\n\\nOption B: Medial preoptic area\xa0regulates bladder contraction, heart rate and blood pressure.\\n\\nOption D: Lateral hypothalamic area is responsible for causing thirst and eating. It also stimulates rage and fighting.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":9,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A lady presents with vascular injury in the area supplied by the middle cerebral artery. Which of the following functional areas of the brain is least likely to be affected?","options":["Wernicke\'s area","Primary Auditory area","Primary visual area","Broca\'s area"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Primary visual area\\n\\nPrimary visual area is supplied by the posterior cerebral artery and hence is least likely to be affected here.\\n\\nA vascular injury to the middle cerebral artery affects the lateral area of cortex mainly.\xa0All the other options in the question are supplied by the middle cerebral artery.\\n\\nThe blood supply of the brain:\\n\\n• Blood is mainly supplied by Internal Carotid artery and vertebrobasilar arteries.\\n• Cortical supply is mainly by cortical arteries (Anterior, Middle and Posterior).\\n\\nAnterior Cerebral Artery\\n\\n• Course - Curves around the corpus callosum within the sagittal fissure\\n• Supply - Dorsomedial frontal and parietal lobes\\n\\nMiddle Cerebral Artery\\n\\n• Course - Runs within Sylvian fissure\\n• Supply - Lateral convexity of the cerebral cortex\\n\\nPosterior Cerebral Artery\\n\\n• Course - Curves behind the midbrain\\n• Supply - Occipital lobe and inferior and medial temporal lobe","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/28630223e8e478787555.jpg"}]},{"n":10,"subject":"Physiology","text":"Beta hCG is structurally similar to which biochemical moiety?","options":["FSH","Oxytocin","ACTH","LH"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. LH\\n\\nThe Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (hCG) has a biological activity similar to that of Luteinizing Hormone (LH).\\n\\nhCG is a glycoprotein\xa0produced by syncytiotrophoblast.\xa0It is made up of\xa0α and\xa0β subunits. Though the\xa0α subunit of hCG is similar to the\xa0α subunits of LH, FSH, and TSH,\xa0the\xa0β subunit has structural similarity to LH only. Hence, LH is a better answer than FSH.\\n\\nhCG is primarily luteinizing and luteotrophic. It appears to act on the same receptor as that of LH. The function of hCG is to prevent regression of the\xa0corpus luteum.\xa0It can be detected in blood as early as 6 days after conception and forms the basis of various tests used to identify pregnancy.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":11,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A patient with impaired RFTs was given a substance \'x\' whose clearance is 20mL/min and the urinary concentration is 100mg/mL. If the urine flow is 2mL/min, calculate the plasma concentration of the substance \'x\'.\\n\\nRFT - Renal function test","options":["200mg/mL","100mg/mL","20mg/mL","10mg/mL"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 10mg/mL\\n\\nThe calculated plasma concentration of the substance \'x\'\xa0in the above patient is 10mg/mL.\\n\\nIt is calculated using the formula -\\n\\nC = [U]x x\xa0V / [P]x\\n\\nWhere,\\n\\n• C is the clearance (mL/min) = 20mL/min\\n• [U]x is the urine concentration of substance x (mg/mL) = 100mg/mL\\n• V is the urine flow rate (mL/min) = 2mL/min\\n• [P]x\xa0is the\xa0plasma concentration of substance x (mg/ml)\\n\\nThe steps of calculation are shown in the image below -","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/055b372c8fc367770620.jpg"}]},{"n":12,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A student was asked to record ECG by using bipolar limb leads. The below image shows the arrangement of the bipolar electrodes and the potentials recorded across lead I and lead II. What is the potential recorded across lead III?","options":["+0.7 mV","+1.7 mV","-1.7 mV","-0.7 mV"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. +0.7 mV\\n\\nAccording to\xa0Einthoven’s law, the potential recorded across lead III is\xa0+0.7 mV\\n\\nEinthoven’s Law:\xa0Einthoven’s law states that if the ECGs are recorded simultaneously with the three limb leads, the sum of the potentials recorded in leads I and III will equal the potential in lead II.\\n\\nLead I potential + Lead III potential = Lead II potential\\n\\nLead III = Lead II – Lead I = (1.2-0.5) = +0.7 mV\\n\\nNote: Einthoven\'s triangle is an imaginary triangle drawn around the area of the heart. It is formed by the arrangement of three bipolar limb leads (the two arms and left leg)\\n\\nECG leads :\\n\\nLA – Left arm: RA – Right arm: LL – Left leg: LF – Left foot\\n\\n• Limb leads measure the electrical activity in the frontal plane\\n• Chest leads measure the electrical activity in the horizontal plane\\n\\nLimb Lead\\nLocation\\nAugmented limb leads\\nLocation\\nChest leads\\nLocation\\n\\n+\\n-\\n\\nI\\nLA\\n\\nRA\\naVF\\nLF +ve\\nV1\\n4th intercostal space. just to the right of the sternum\\n\\nII\\nLL\\n\\nRA\\naVL\\nLA +ve\\nV2\\n4th intercostal space. just to the left of the sternum\\n\\nIII\\nLL\\n\\nLA\\naVR\\nRA +ve\\nV3\\nmidway between V2 and V4\\n\\nV4\\nMidclavicular line. fifth intercostal\\n\\nV5\\nAnterior axillary line, same level as V4\\n\\nV6\\nMidaxillary line, the same level as V4 and V5","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/98adb857d031ade75132.jpg"]},{"n":13,"subject":"Physiology","text":"Urine flow rate is 10 mL/min; plasma inulin is 2 mg/mL; and urine inulin is 25 mg/mL.Which of the following is true assuming GFR to be normal?","options":["Inulin clearance > GFR","GFR cannot be calculated","Inulin clearance < GFR","Inulin clearance = GFR"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Inulin clearance = GFR\\n\\nInulin clearance is calculated by the formula:\\n\\nSubstituting the values given,\\n\\n• Uinulin= 25 mg/mL\\n• Vinulin= 10 mL/min\\n• Pinulin=2 mg/mL\\n\\nThus, Inulin clearance=GFR (normal GFR= 125 ml/min)\\n\\nNOTE:\\n\\nInulin is a polymer of fructose,\xa0clearance of inulin can be used for measurement of GFR, because of the following reasons:\\n\\n• Freely filtered\\n• Neither reabsorbed nor secreted in the tubules\\n• Nontoxic\\n• Not metabolized by the body.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/6e1b9f0a8b6e4e0443a3.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/55f8cb23c435fb556a2c.png"}]},{"n":14,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A 28-year-old woman was detected with a positive antibody titer against the thyroid peroxidase enzyme. All of the following steps are catalyzed by this enzyme for the synthesis of thyroid hormones except?","options":["Oxidation","Coupling","Organification","Iodide trapping"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Iodide trapping\\n\\nIodide trapping\xa0(iodide uptake) is not\xa0catalyzed by thyroid peroxidase. Oxidation, organification, and coupling are catalyzed by it.\\n\\nSteps in synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormone:\\n\\n• Iodide uptake:\xa0The basolateral sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) actively takes up\xa0I-\xa0(iodide) from the circulation into the follicular cells. The process of concentrating iodide in the cell is known as iodide trapping. Iodide is then transported into the colloid through a pendrin (chloride-iodide) transporter.\\n• Thyroglobulin synthesis: Thyroglobulin (Tg), is a large glycoprotein rich in tyrosine residues. It is synthesized by the thyrocytes and secreted into colloid.\\n• Iodination of thyroglobulin: This is regulated by the\xa0thyroid peroxidase (TPO) enzyme. TPO has three functions:\\n\\n• Oxidation: TPO oxidizes iodine\xa0in the presence of H2O2\\n• Organification: TPO links tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin with iodine (I2) to form monoiodotyrosine (MIT), diiodotyrosine (DIT)\\n• Coupling reactions: TPO combines iodinated tyrosine residues to make\xa0MIT + DIT = T3 (triiodothyronine),\xa0DIT + DIT = T4 (tetraiodothyronine)\\n\\n• Storage: Thyroid hormones are bound to thyroglobulin and stored in the follicular lumen as colloids. The colloid has hormone reserves for 2-3 months\\n• Release:\xa0Iodinated thyroglobulin is cleaved by lysosomal proteolytic enzymes in the colloid from the thyroid cells. This releases thyroxine and triiodothyronine into the peripheral blood.\\n• Peripheral T3 conversion: T4 undergoes deiodination in extrathyroidal tissue to form T3, which is the active form of the hormone.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e8670f6c434b4d1522cd.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Physiology","text":"An elderly patient was diagnosed with ischemic heart disease and was started on beta-blockers. However, the patient continued to have symptoms despite it\'s use. He was then started on ivabradine. This drug improves the symptoms of angina by which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Decreasing the preload","Decreasing the cardiac contractility","Decreasing the oxygen demand","Increasing the coronary blood flow"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Decreasing the oxygen demand\\n\\nIvabradine decreases the automaticity in the sinoatrial node and thus reduces the heart rate. This reduction in the heart rate decreases the myocardial oxygen demand.\\n\\nThe oxygen requirement of the heart mainly depends on the work done by the heart. This work mainly depends on cardiac output and peripheral resistance. An increase in these parameters will increase the oxygen requirement of the heart.\\n\\nImportant determinants of cardiac output are:\\n\\nStroke volume- It is the amount of blood pumped out of the heart during each heartbeat. This stroke volume increases whenever, there is increased venous return to the heart, increased end-diastolic volume (preload), and increased contractility. Contractility further depends on the initial length of the cardiac muscle fiber and the autonomic innervation.\\n\\nHeart rate- Increased heart rate increases the cardiac output which in turn increases the oxygen requirement. Heart rate\xa0is controlled primarily by the autonomic nerves, with sympathetic stimulation increasing the rate and parasympathetic stimulation decreasing the rate.\\n\\nAfterload- The tension at which the load is lifted is called afterload. As the afterload increases, the heart has to work more in order to pump blood to the organs. This in turn results in increased oxygen requirement. It is dependent on the peripheral resistance.\\n\\nDrugs used in the management of angina either decrease the work done by the heart or increases the blood flow to the heart.\\n\\n• Nitrates- They increase the cGMP and cause venodilation. It mainly results in an increase in venous capacitance and a decrease in the ventricular preload. They might also increase coronary flow in some areas and in variant anginas.\\n• Calcium channel blockers- Blocking the calcium channels leads to the relaxation of vascular smooth muscles and decreased impulses in the conduction system. This leads to decreased peripheral resistance,\xa0decreased blood pressure, decreased contractility, decreased heart rate, and decreased oxygen requirement of the heart.\\n• Beta-blockers- They decrease the oxygen requirement by decreasing the\xa0heart rate, blood pressure, and contractility. Some drugs like carvedilol and nebivolol also cause vasodilation.\\n\\nNewer antianginal drugs\\n\\n• Ranolazine-\xa0It inhibits the late sodium current and reduces the intracellular calcium concentration. It reduces diastolic tension, cardiac contractility, and work.\\n• Trimetazidine- inhibits the fatty acid oxidation in the myocardium. It decreases the oxygen requirement of the heart.\\n• Ivabradine-\xa0decreases the heart rate and decreases oxygen requirement.\\n• Fasudil-\xa0It is a Rho kinase inhibitor. It is useful in coronary vasospasm.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Physiology","text":"Match the following hormones to their respective receptor\'s target of action.\\n\\nHormone\\nTarget\\n\\n1. Thyroid hormone\\nA. Receptor tyrosine kinase\\n\\n2. Insulin\\nB. Adenylyl cyclase\\n\\n3. Atrial natriuretic peptide\\nC. Intracellular receptor\\n\\n4. Norepinephrine\\nD. Guanylyl cyclase","options":["1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A","1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D","1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B","1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B\\n\\nThe correct option is\xa0Option 3: 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B\\n\\n1. Thyroid hormones\\nC. Intracellular receptor\\n\\n2. Insulin\\nA. Receptor tyrosine kinase\\n\\n3. Atrial natriuretic peptide\\nD. Guanylyl cyclase\\n\\n4. Norepinephrine\\nB. Adenylyl cyclase\\n\\nThyroid hormones act on intracellular receptors (intracytoplasmic or intranuclear) to induce the transcription of selected mRNAs, thereby promoting protein synthesis. Other hormones with similar action include steroid hormones and retinoic acid.\\n\\nInsulin\xa0activates the tyrosine kinase enzyme on the intracellular (cytoplasmic) portions of transmembrane receptors, thereby phosphorylating the receptor. Other hormones with similar action include EGF, PDGF, M-CSF\\n\\nAtrial natriuretic peptide\xa0increases intracellular cGMP by activating the enzyme guanylyl cyclase. A similar mechanism of action is seen with nitric oxide (NO).\\n\\nNorepinephrine\xa0acts on intracellular adenylyl cyclase enzyme via beta-1 (adenylyl cyclase activation to increase cAMP) and alpha-2 (adenylyl cyclase inhibition to decrease cAMP). It activates phospholipase C and increases the production of DAG, IP3, via alpha-1 receptors.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"Physiology","text":"Which among the following options depict the correct match between the substances (column A) and their site of production (column B)?\\n\\nColumn A\\nColumn B\\n\\ni. 1, 25 dihydroxycholecalciferol\\na. Liver\\n\\nii. Erythropoietin\\nb. Proximal tubule cells\\n\\niii. Angiotensinogen\\nc. Juxtaglomerular cells\\n\\niv. Renin\\nd. Interstitial cells in peritubular capillaries","options":["(i-a), (ii- b), (iii-c), (iv-d)","(i-b), (ii-d), (iii-a), (iv-c)","(i-a), (ii-d), (iii-c), (iv-b)","(i-c), (ii-b), (iii-a), (iv-d)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. (i-b), (ii-d), (iii-a), (iv-c)\\n\\nThe correct match is\xa0(i-b), (ii-d), (iii-a), (iv-c).\\n\\nHormones produced by the kidney are:\\n\\n1. Renin- It is produced by juxtaglomerular cells. It converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. Factors that affect renin secretion are:\\n\\nStimulatory\\nInhibitory\\n\\nIncreased sympathetic activity\\n\\nIncreased afferent arteriolar pressure\\n\\nIncreased circulating catecholamines\\n\\nAngiotensin II\\n\\nProstaglandins\\nIncreased Na+ and Cl− reabsorption across macula densa\\n\\nVasopressin\\n\\nConditions those increase renin secretion are:\\n\\n• Na+ depletion\\n• Diuretics\\n• Hypotension\\n• Hemorrhage\\n• Upright posture\\n• Dehydration\\n• Cardiac failure\\n• Cirrhosis\\n• Constriction of renal artery or aorta\\n\\n2. Erythropoietin- the majority of the erythropoietin is produced from\xa0interstitial cells in the peritubular capillary bed of the kidneys. Some amount is produced by the\xa0perivenous hepatocytes in the liver. It is released in response to hypoxia, cobalt salts, and androgens. It increases red cell production.\\n\\n3. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol-\xa0The 25-hydroxycholecalciferol produced in the liver is further converted to 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol by the proximal tubular cells. This is also called calcitriol. It increases the calcium reabsorption from the kidneys, intestine, and increases the activity of osteoblasts.\\n\\nNote- Angiotensinogen is synthesized in the liver. It is converted to angiotensin I by renin.\xa0Its circulating level is increased by glucocorticoids, thyroid hormones, estrogens, several cytokines, and angiotensin II.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":18,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A newly joined trainee at the gymnasium was able to lift about 8 Kilograms of weight using both his upper limbs. When two more kilograms were added, he could not resist and had dropped the weight down. Which receptor and nerve fiber are responsible for his inability to bear the weight?","options":["Golgi tendon organ and Ib fiber","Golgi tendon organ and Ia fiber","Muscle spindle and Ib fiber","Muscle spindle and Ia fiber"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Golgi tendon organ and Ib fiber\\n\\nThe Golgi tendon organ and Ib fiber are responsible for his inability to bear the weight.\\n\\nHere, in the scenario given, two types of physiological reflexes have happened.\\n\\n• Stretch reflex/myotatic reflex - This has been responsible for the ability to bear the initial 8 kgs of weight by the trainee.\\n• Inverse myotatic/ Inverse stretch reflex - When two more kgs have been added, this has brought the inhibition of motor neuron discharge to his muscles which are responsible for his inability to bear the weight.\\n\\nGolgi tendon reflex (Inverse myotatic/ Inverse stretch reflex)\xa0is\xa0disynaptic,\xa0is the opposite of the stretch reflex.\\n\\nWhen the tension on the muscle becomes extreme, the inhibitory effect from the Golgi tendon organ, carried by Ib afferents, leads to instantaneous relaxation of the entire muscle. This effect is called the\xa0lengthening reaction.\\n\\nIt is a protective mechanism to prevent tearing of the muscle or avulsion of the tendon from its attachments to the bone.\\n\\nNote: Muscle spindles senses changes in muscle length (stretch).","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0a5cb7d67c2000eff921.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/02802dfd54f1a02fe5d3.jpg"}]},{"n":19,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A patient with diabetes mellitus for the past 5 years presents with vomiting and abdominal pain. On examination, she appears dehydrated. Investigations revealed pH - 7.00, blood sugar level- 500 mg/dL, and ketonuria. What type of breathing pattern can be observed in this case?","options":["Deep, gasping inspiration with a pause at full inspiration followed by a brief insufficient release.","Regular deep respirations with periods of apnea","Increased volume of respiration","Deep and rapid breathing pattern"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Deep and rapid breathing pattern\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario with severe\xa0dehydration, hyperglycemia, ketonuria,\xa0and\xa0metabolic acidosis(pH-7.00) is suggestive of diabetic ketoacidosis. The type of breathing pattern that can be seen in a case of diabetic ketoacidosis is a deep and rapid breathing pattern (Kussmaul breathing).\\n\\nNormally, the H+ ions from acids in our body are buffered with various mechanisms. But in cases where acid production is high like in diabetic ketoacidosis and sepsis, the buffering capacity is exceeded. The resulting acidosis stimulates respiration and decreases alveolar CO2 in order to produce a compensatory fall in the H+ ions.\\n\\nDiabetic ketoacidosis is the most serious complication of type 1 diabetes mellitus. The triad of DKA includes hyperglycemia, ketosis, and metabolic acidosis. Clinical manifestations of diabetic ketoacidosis include dehydration, tachypnea with deep, sighing respiration (Kussmaul breathing), nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, confusion, and drowsiness.\\n\\nPatients with DKA typically have:\\n\\n• Hyperglycemia (blood glucose >250 mg/dl)\\n• Venous pH < 7.3 or serum bicarbonate <18 mmol/L\\n• Ketonemia or ketonuria\\n\\nSigns of ketoacidosis:\\n\\n• Kussmaul\xa0breathing (deep, rapid breathing)\\n• Fruity breath odor (acetone)\\n• Prolonged Q-T interval\\n• Diminished neurocognitive function\\n• Coma\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Apneusis is characterized by\xa0deep, gasping inspiration with a pause at full inspiration followed by a brief insufficient release. It is seen in mid-pontine level lesions with cut vagi.\\n\\nOption B: Biot\'s breathing is characterized by regular deep respirations with periods of apnea. It occurs due to pontine damage by stroke, trauma, or uncal herniation.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Hyperpnea refers to an increased volume of respiration with or without an increased rate of breathing.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":20,"subject":"Physiology","text":"An EEG conducted during sleep in a normal individual is given below. Identify the most likely change in neurotransmitters in the highlighted period.\\n\\nEEG: Electroencephalogram","options":["Increase in norepinephrine","Increase in serotonin","Increase in histamine","Increase in acetylcholine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Increase in acetylcholine\\n\\nIn the given EEG, waves highlighted in red show the typical saw-tooth appearance of rapid eye movement (REM) sleep. The most likely change in neurotransmitters during this period is an increase in acetylcholine.\\n\\nThe other change in neurotransmitters during REM sleep is\xa0a decrease in serotonin and norepinephrine.\\n\\nAn EEG is a recording that represents the electrical activity of the brain. It is used in localizing neuropathological lesions.\\n\\nThe brainstem ascending arousal system is composed of several groups of neurons like:\\n\\n• Locus coeruleus secrets norepinephrine\\n• Raphe nucleus\xa0 secretes serotonin\\n• Pontine nucleus secretes acetylcholine\\n\\nDuring wakefulness, the locus coeruleus and raphe nucleus are dominant and the pontine is suppressed. Therefore, when a person is awake the norepinephrine and serotonin are increased and acetylcholine is decreased. There is also decreased GABA and increased histamine during wakefulness.\\n\\nREM sleep occurs when the pontine nucleus is dominant and locus coeruleus and raphe nucleus are suppressed. Therefore acetylcholine is increased and norepinephrine and serotonin are decreased.\\n\\nNREM (non-rapid eye movement) sleep occurs when there is suppression of the thalamus and cortex due to an increase in GABA and a decrease in histamine.\\n\\nOther options: Increase in serotonin, norepinephrine, and histamine is seen during wakefulness.\\n\\nThe image given below shows EEG patterns in different stages of sleep.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/c49b3617dfcf958b27de.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/524c72f7df6f3202fd31.jpg"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A pressure-volume diagram of the left ventricle is given below. Calculate the stroke volume considering the heart rate as 65 beats per minute.","options":["50 mL","70 mL","100 mL","120 mL"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 70 mL\\n\\nThe stroke volume of this pressure-volume diagram is 70 mL.\\n\\nThe points in the graph indicate the following events of the cardiac cycle:\\n\\n• Point 1: Opening of the mitral valve. The corresponding reading on the graph denotes end-systolic volume (ESV).\\n• Point 2: Closing of the mitral valve.\xa0The corresponding reading on the graph denotes end-diastolic volume (EDV).\\n• Point 3: Opening of the aortic valve.\\n• Point 4: Closing of the aortic valve.\\n\\nStroke volume is the amount of blood that the heart pumps out with each beat. It is about 70 mL from each ventricle in\xa0resting conditions.\\n\\nIn a pressure-volume diagram, stroke volume (SV) can be calculated using the values of ESV (50 mL) and EDV (120 mL):\\n\\nStroke volume = End diastolic volume\xa0– End systolic volume\\n\\nTherefore, SV = 120 - 50 = 70 mL.\\n\\nCardiac output\xa0can be calculated from the given data using the formula: \xa0Cardiac output = Stroke volume x heart rate\\n\\nTherefore, in this case, cardiac output = 70 x 65 = 4550 mL.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/b04c74928816464dd887.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3982ced2d636c41fcb89.png"}]},{"n":22,"subject":"Physiology","text":"The image below depicts which of the following types of cell-to-cell signaling?","options":["Paracrine","Autocrine","Endocrine","Merocrine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Paracrine\\n\\nThe given image shows the\xa0paracrine cell-to-cell signaling mechanism. In paracrine signaling, the\xa0signaling molecules released by a cell only affect target cells in close proximity to it.\\n\\nAn example of paracrine signaling is the release of\xa0endothelin-1,\xa0a potent vasoconstrictor released by the endothelial cells.\\n\\nSynaptic signaling\xa0is a special case of paracrine signaling (for chemical synapses) between neurons and target cells.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: In the\xa0autocrine signaling\xa0mechanism, the cells respond to the signaling molecules that are released by themselves, which is the site of release and action within the same cell. Note that the signaling molecules are first secreted out of the cell and then act on the same cell through receptors. An example is the proliferation of activated T-cells by IL-2 released by the same cell after it interacts with antigen-presenting cells.\\n\\nOption C: In\xa0endocrine signaling,\xa0a signaling molecule, a hormone (for example insulin, FSH, LH), is usually carried by the blood from its site of release to act on its target at a distance.\\n\\nThe below image shows the different types of extracellular cell-to-cell communication mechanisms.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Merocrine is a type of glandular secretion and not an extracellular signaling mechanism.\xa0In this, the\xa0secretions\xa0are poured out by\xa0exocytosis and the\xa0cell remains intact. An\xa0example\xa0of the merocrine gland is the goblet cell and eccrine sweat gland.\\n\\nAn illustration depicting this mechanism is given below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/248ed402717d74ba2132.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e55218c48adc4bb4e1fa.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9826e1c599af9c5a9add.jpg"}]},{"n":23,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A 65-year-old-lady with CKD presents with bilateral limb muscle weakness and fasciculations. On investigation, her serum potassium level was found to be 6 mEq/L. What is the likely mechanism to have caused her clinical features?","options":["Resting membrane potential of nerves is hyperpolarised","Resting membrane potential is reduced","Resting membrane potential moved away from action potential threshold","Resting membrane potential moved closer to the threshold potential"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Resting membrane potential moved closer to the threshold potential\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario along with the serum potassium levels of 6 mEq/L (normal levels: 3.5-5) is suggestive of hyperkalemia.\xa0Resting membrane potential moved closer to the threshold potential is the likely mechanism to have caused her clinical features.\\n\\nResting membrane potential (RMP) is the difference in the electrical potentials between the cytoplasm of the cell and the extracellular space.\\n\\nAt rest, there are more open K+ channels compared to Na+ channels, thus the membrane permeability of K+ is greater. As a result of this, the intracellular and extracellular K+ concentrations are the major determinants of the RMP. Furthermore, the RMP is close to the equilibrium potential of K+ in general.\\n\\nIn hyperkalemia, the extracellular level of K+ is increased. Thus the RMP moves closer to the threshold to elicit an action potential leading to a more excitable nerve.\xa0The excitable nerve, stimulates the muscle repeatedly causing fasciculations, exhaustion of muscle, and weakness.\\n\\nIn hypokalemia, the extracellular level of K+ is reduced. Thus the RMP is reduced and the neuron is hyperpolarized.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":24,"subject":"Physiology","text":"In ECG tracings, sharp irregular spikes of the baseline that are particularly prominent in the limb leads are typically seen in","options":["Patient with hypothyroidism","Recording done with paper speed of 50 mm/s","Recording done with paper speed of 12.5 mm/s","Patient that is not relaxed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Patient that is not relaxed\\n\\nSharp irregular spikes of the baseline that are particularly prominent in the limb leads are seen in a patient that is not relaxed.\\n\\nThough the ECG recorders are configured to pick up the electrical frequency generated by the heart better, they also detect the contractions of skeletal muscles. So, unless the patient is relaxed and lying down comfortably, the tracing may show some skeletal muscle activity.\\n\\nEx:-\\n\\nA patient that is shivering due to cold, or one that has involuntary movements due to conditions like Parkinson\'s, etc would cause spikes in the baseline of the ECG.\\n\\nIn the case of shivering, the spikes would be more exaggerated and synchronized\xa0than that seen in a patient that is not adequately relaxed, because of the skeletal muscle groups contracting together in case of shivering.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\n• Patients with hypothyroidism would show bradycardia, low voltage QRS complexes, and widespread T-wave inversions.\\n• The recording is normally done with a paper speed of 25 mm/s.\\n\\n• Recording at 12.5 mm/s, which is slower than normal, would cause QRS complex that are close together\xa0(creating the false impression of a rapid heart rate) and P waves, QRS complexes, and T waves that are narrow and spiky\\n• Recording at 50 mm/s, which is faster than normal, would produce longer intervals between QRS complexes (creating the false impression of a slow heart rate), widened QRS complexes, and an apparently long QT interval.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":25,"subject":"Physiology","text":"An agglutination test was done to identify blood groups in several people. Subjects with which of the following findings are called universal blood recipients?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 2\\n\\nThe agglutination test in image 2 corresponds to the AB-positive blood group. The AB-positive blood group is the universal recipient type.\\n\\nBlood typing or blood matching is done before transfusions to avoid incompatibility. Red blood cells (RBC) are first separated from plasma and diluted with saline solution for an agglutination test. Anti-A and anti-B agglutinins are added to different portions. If RBCs are clumped/agglutinated, then an antigen-antibody reaction has occurred.\\n\\nAntigens A and B are seen on the surfaces of the red blood cells. Depending on inheritance, people have one or both on the surface of their cells. When neither of them is present, the blood type is O. Based on the antigens present, blood is classified into four main types: O (OO), A (OA/AA), B (OB/BB), and AB.\\n\\nWhen the A or B agglutinins are absent in the body, corresponding anti-agglutinins (anti-A or anti-B) are found in the plasma. Those with AB blood group have no circulating anti-agglutinins and do not develop transfusion reactions due to ABO incompatibility. Therefore, people with type AB blood are called universal recipients. They are also considered universal plasma donors.\\n\\nRh type system is also essential for blood transfusion. The difference with the A-B-O system is that the antibodies to Rh antigen in people without Rh antigen occur after exposure and not spontaneously.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: The given agglutination test is suggestive of O positive blood type.\\n\\nOption C: The test result shows agglutination with anti-A agglutinin and suggests blood type A negative.\\n\\nOption D: The given test result shows no agglutination and suggests blood type O negative. People with blood type O negative are considered as universal donors.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/53530871f643fa59594d.png"]},{"n":26,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A person has consumed a meal rich in protein. Which of the following substances would primarily be secreted that would cause an increase in the secretion of acid in the stomach?","options":["Histamine","Secretin","Cholecystokinin","Gastrin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Gastrin\\n\\nWhen a meal rich in protein has been consumed, the\xa0increase in the secretion of acid in the stomach is likely due to\xa0gastrin.\\n\\nThe peptides\xa0and amino acids\xa0have a special stimulatory effect on the gastric cells located in the pyloric antrum, causing the secretion of gastrin. This further increases the hydrochloric acid secretion.\\n\\nStimuli that increase acid and gastrin secretion:\\n\\n• Distension of stomach\\n• Peptides\\n• Calcium\\n• Increased vagal discharge\\n\\nStimuli that decrease acid and gastrin secretion:\\n\\n• Gastric acid\\n• Secretin family - secretin, glucagon, VIP, GIP\\n• Somatostatin\\n• Calcitonin","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":27,"subject":"Physiology","text":"What is the next step in excitation-contraction coupling after the power stroke in skeletal muscle contraction?","options":["Attachment of ATP to myosin head","Dissociation of ADP and Pi","Cessation of calcium release from sarcoplasm","Conformational change in Troponin T"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Attachment of ATP to myosin head\\n\\nIn excitation-contraction coupling, the next step after the power stroke is the\xa0attachment of ATP to the myosin head.\\n\\nMuscle contraction occurs by sliding the thin filament over the thick filament. The sequence of events is as follows:\\n\\n• Troponin I is bound to actin and tropomyosin. In a resting muscle, it\xa0covers the sites where actin interacts with myosin heads. The myosin head contains a tightly bound adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and an inorganic phosphate (Pi).\\n• Following an action potential,\xa0Calcium ions (Ca2+) are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum and\xa0cytosolic Ca2+ is increased.\xa0Simultaneously\xa0Ca2+ influx also happens through the voltage-gated Ca2+ channels.\\n• The free Ca2+ binds with\xa0troponin C. This binding will weaken the troponin I interaction with actin and exposes the actin-binding site for myosin.\\n• This results in the formation of myosin-actin cross-bridges.\\n• The\xa0cross-bridge between the head of myosin and the active site of actin causes a conformational change in the head. This leads to the\xa0power stroke, which moves the thin filament relative to the thick filament.\\n• A new ATP molecule binds to the free site on myosin. The ATP is further hydrolyzed to\xa0ADP and phosphate\xa0ions.\xa0The binding of the new ATP molecule\xa0to the free site on\xa0myosin causes\xa0detachment of the head of myosin\xa0from actin.\\n• This causes a re-cocking\xa0of myosin head which completes the cycle.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/166917312c94874552ab.jpg"}]},{"n":28,"subject":"Physiology","text":"Spermatogenesis is maintained by which hormone?","options":["Testosterone","FSH","LH","All of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. All of the above\\n\\nAll three of them (testosterone, FSH, LH) are required for spermatogenesis.\\n\\nPulses of hypothalamic GnRH stimulate the anterior pituitary to secrete FSH and LH, which then act on the testes\\n\\nFSH acts on the Sertoli cells to stimulate spermatogenesis and inhibin secretion. LH acts on the Leydig cells to stimulate testosterone secretion. Testosterone, acting locally on the Sertoli cells, is essential for maintaining spermatogenesis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Physiology","text":"A normal healthy adult participated in a respiratory study that was conducted to understand the respiratory functions. In the upright position, an increase in all of the following parameters can be seen with respect to the base of the lungs except?","options":["Ventilation-perfusion ratio","Ventilation per unit volume","Intrapleural pressure","Pulmonary blood flow"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Ventilation-perfusion ratio\\n\\nIn the upright position, an increase in the\xa0ventilation-perfusion ratio (V/Q ratio)\xa0is not seen with respect to the base of the lungs.\xa0At the base of the lung, the\xa0V/Q ratio is\xa0decreased.\\n\\nV/Q ratio\xa0is the ratio of alveolar ventilation (V) to pulmonary blood flow (Q) for the lungs at rest. The average value of the V/Q ratio throughout the lungs is 0.8.\xa0When this ratio is mismatched, it can cause CO2 retention (when solely, ventilation is impaired) or lowered arterial O2 (when perfusion is impaired).\\n\\nNormally, in the upright position, the V/Q ratio in the normal lungs markedly undergoes changes from the apex to the base due to the effect of gravity as shown below -\\n\\nAt the base of the lung:\\n\\nBoth ventilation and perfusion increase since the flow is towards gravity.\\n\\nIncreased ventilation is due to the increase in intrapleural pressure compared to the apex, thereby\xa0making the intrapulmonary intrapleural pressure difference less at the base. Thus, due to this low-pressure difference, the lung is less expanded compared to the apex. This makes the ventilation per unit volume rise as the lung expansion is relatively small.\\n\\nThe alveolar pressure also decreases thus reducing the compression of the vessels and increasing the pulmonary blood flow at the base. An increase in perfusion is more than an increase in ventilation, i.e., denominator increases. Hence, the\xa0ratio decreases.\\n\\nAt the apex (against gravity):\\n\\nBoth ventilation and perfusion decrease since the flow is against gravity.\\n\\nThe reason for decreased ventilation is that at the start of inspiration, intrapleural pressure is more negative thus making the intrapulmonary intrapleural pressure difference increase at the apex. The apex is above the level of the heart and capillary pressure at the apex is close to atmospheric pressure thus decreasing the blood flow. But, a\xa0decrease in perfusion is higher than a decrease in ventilation. Hence, the\xa0ratio increases.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/79f69e31535856ae1de2.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/58d596d1afa9a3986a72.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Physiology","text":"The fourth heart sound is caused by:","options":["Vibrations in the ventricular wall during systole","Closure of aortic and pulmonary valves","Ventricular filling","Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ventricular filling\\n\\nThe fourth heart sound is caused by ventricular filling. The atrial contraction against the stiff ventricle produces an audible S4 sound\\n\\nDuring ventricular filling, the atria contract to push blood into the ventricles. In conditions where the ventricular walls are stiff or hypertrophied, such as in conditions like hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or systemic hypertension, the atrial contraction against the stiff ventricle produces an audible S4 sound.\\n\\nDuring the cardiac cycle, there are two main phases: diastole and systole. Diastole is the relaxation phase when the chambers fill with blood, while systole is the contraction phase when the chambers pump blood out.\\n\\nThe fourth heart sound (S4) occurs during diastole, specifically late diastole, just before the first heart sound (S1). It is caused by atrial contraction, which happens as the atria forcefully contract to push the remaining blood into the non-compliant or hypertrophied ventricle.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4650d4e5a5205044fa7a.jpg"}]}]}')}}]);